ion but  they have a lower volume  um two to three mills compared to four  to ten mils so they have about  20 to 25 percent less vacuum so they  don't pull in as much blood  um into the tube and they were able to  show that they reduced the blood loss  from 11 ml to 6 ml per day  there was an overall 45 reduction in  blood loss  and they didn't find any barriers to  implementation in terms of  amongst the nurses or the lab staff so  there was a lot of  satisfaction with this process and so  i think this is something that can be  looked at and probably should be looked  at at your institution in terms of what  types  of tubes you're using for your  laboratory testing  okay we're going to move to the final  topic which is  prioritize using blood wisely  so there have been  many studies again that have looked at  restrictive  versus liberal transfusion and  have not shown any difference in terms  of outcomes with respect to  mortality and this is a slide  from one of my colleagues that  summarized some of the  observational studies that have been  done these are all surgical studies  where they looked at patients undergoing  the specific types of surgeries  and compared those patients who were  transfused versus those patients who did  not receive a transfusion  and looked at their 30-day mortality and  you can see across the board  there was increased mortality associated  with transfusion  and so over time we have recognized that  it's  really important to think about  restrictive transfusion  so that we are providing  appropriate care to our patients so when  possible  transfusing don't transfuse more than  one unit at a time  in a non-bleeding hemodynamically stable  patient  and then the other is really looking at  transfusion threshold so don't transfuse  red cells  in asymptomatic non-bleeding patients  with a hemoglobin greater than  70 or a higher threshold for patients  with active cardiovascular disease  and so there are actually you know over  eight to ten  societies again that have also included  these in their choosing wisely  statements  in terms of restrictive transfusion  so again you know what are the  interventions that we can put into place  um to think a little bit about how we  improve practice um so this was a  recent review of 84 studies looking at  behavioral interventions to reduce red  cell transfusion  and you can see on the right there were  things like education guidelines and  reminders  which unfortunately alone did not cause  any great  change in terms of uh reducing  transfusion  but only when you had um in addition to  that  either adding decision support to  um for example computer provider order  entry  or prospective screening and audit or  different types of policy interventions  or the greatest decrease which was  combining all of these  components or different various  components that's when you saw that  there was actually an improvement  in red cell transfusion so i wanted to  tell you a little bit about some of the  things that we've done  locally unfortunately not all systems  can  incorporate clinical decision support  systems or have  electronic patient records i think we're  all moving in that direction and so i  think for sure  i'm building those order processes  in to help guide the clinician in terms  of  ordering transfusion is important but  what we've done here  is a little bit more in terms of  empowering the medical labor  technologists  within the lab to sort of help with  the oversight and ensuring that  transfusion's used appropriately  so this was one of our initial studies  looking at  improving transfusion at a single site  we partnered with them um in 2013  uh with our local site sunnybrook and  university health network um education  on the guidelines was  introduced and then we introduced um  perspective order screening where  the medical laboratory technologists  would actually screen each of the orders  and determine whether or not they were  um outside of guidelines  if they were outside of guidelines then  that would be escalated to the  transfusion physician  on call to help have a discussion with  the ordering physician  and so the medical advisory council  approved this process which was really  important to help set the stage for  um how the transfusion culture and how  blood should be transfused at lake ridge  and then over time we went to 24  7 screening across the  the week days and weekends and you can  see with that that  um the number of red cells transfused  over time  decreased by a third so much so that we  needed to  adjust the inventory and there was uh  in terms of a balancing measure we used  a hospital death which didn't change  over the time of implementation of  this program  and so this has also been rolled out on  a  larger scale with the start study so  this was  looking at screening by technologists to  reduce  transfusion and so again use the very  similar type of intervention  which was multimodal and that was  ensuring that guidelines were in place  and this was  13 participating hospitals across canada  um there were education sessions that  were provided  not only for the physicians but also for  the technologists in terms of how to  screen orders  there was prospective screening by the  laboratory technologists with approval  before issue of anything any orders that  were suspected to be  not adherent to the guidelines and then  monthly auditing of appropriateness with  feedback to the clinical team  and so with that during this  time period which was 10 months after  the intervention  uh there were 1950 patients that were  audited  with over 2 800 red cells transfused  and the appropriateness of the  transfusions  increased from 73.5 to 85  there was a decrease in the  pre-transfusion hemoglobin  an increase in the single unit  transfusions  across the hospitals and a significant  decrease in the number of red cells  transfused so over these 13 hospitals  there was a decrease of 500 units per  month  and when we looked at a balancing  measure of under transfusion  it was actually pretty low so only about  2.2 percent  of the under transfusion events so those  were events that didn't get a  transfusion even though the hemoglobin  was less than 60  which was not explained by lab era or  clear medical  religious reasons it was pretty unusual  for that to occur and the intervention  did not change the length of stay or the  need for icu support or in hospital  mortality  again in terms of balancing measures  so you know we've brought that into  using blood wisely so  the um last issue in terms or the last  topic  i really wanted to tell you a little bit  about using blood wisely which is  choosing wisely canada's first national  implementation campaign and we've done  that with our national blood supplier  canadian blood services  and the campaign challenges hospitals to  benchmark themselves  on appropriate transfusion practices and  to lead by example through the adoption  of a defined set of  evidence-informed and proven strategies  and so really the aim is to decrease  inappropriate red cell transfusions in  canada  and so i just wanted to show you just a  few screenshots from it but  really we started in september of 2020  and it was just sort of take the  challenge you know do an audit are your  hospitals using blood wisely  um hospitals would do an audit we made  it pretty simple  some hospitals had automated processes  for this some hospitals did it manually  but we  just asked hospitals to do a transfusion  report for about 50 consecutive  inpatients  to identify 50 transfusion episodes and  really looked at inpatient since  our guidelines really apply to  inpatients as opposed to outpatients  um the hospitals looked at their  benchmarks and these ventures were set  as 65  of the red cell transfusion episodes are  single unit  and at least 80 percent of the  transfusions had a pre-transfusion  hemoglobin of 80 or less  so again just accounting for the fact  that there may be bleeding patients who  need  double units triple units or you have  some patients  who who are bleeding or have cardiac  disease and might need higher hemoglobin  thresholds  so um really setting these benchmarks  and then  if they weren't met then really  enrolling in a equality improvement  initiative  to sort of help the hospital move  forward with these  and if they were met then hospitals  received a using blood wisely hospital  designation  and so we designed an intervention  toolbox which had  templates for guidelines and education  and order sets  also some information on prospective  screening  and alternatives to blood and supported  hospitals through this process  with a series of practice webinars to  walk them through these different stages  from the spot audit to planning setting  up their  for success and then actually doing the  intervention and measuring  so i'm really excited to say that since  we started in september we have 192  participating hospitals um as of  june 21st so this is a a little month  behind our  target was to get 200 hospitals signed  up  um we have 58 hospitals that have  achieved their designation of using  blood wisely so they've  met the targets that we've set and as  well as sustain them  and um you know these 192 hospitals um  that are participating at various stages  account for about two-thirds of the red  cells that are transfused in canada  so um you know it's not just small  hospitals that are participating but we  have really  um a wide breadth of hospitals that are  participating  in the uh initiative and so just showing  some  baseline audit data but just you know  percent single unit transfusions on the  x-axis and on the y-axis the percent of  transfusions with  a pre-transfusion hemoglobin of less  than 80. and our goal is to get all  these dots  into the top right corner so just gives  you an idea that there are some that are  already there but there are some that  need some help pushing up towards that  top right corner  so you know what is the value of of  everything that i've talked about today  um you know we've talked about the  sort of different interventions and each  of the pillars for patient blood  management  and and our hope is that the value is to  kind of hopefully emulate some of the  things that have been done  in other countries so this is a study  from western australia that looked at  their  patient blood management programs over  the years  and and they instituted this starting in  2008-2009  so you can see there was a pretty  significant decrease  in terms of the number of red cell units  transfused even though in gray in the  background  is the increase the slow increase in the  population  in that region and so with this um  the the value was shown that they were  able to show over the years  some associations with decreased  in hospital mortality and length of stay  decrease in hospital acquired infections  no increase in acute myocardial  infarction or stroke if anything  decreased although they did have a  slight increase in 28 day readmissions  but the idea is that hopefully with  these patient blood management  programs that encompass many items and  sorry i forgot to say that in  western australia they had you know  multimodal  interventions from appropriate  transfusions to managing iron deficiency  um to minimizing blood loss and surgery  so it was really  all across the board a real complete  patient blood management program that  with these patient blood management  programs we will improve  care so in summary um  i hope i've given you you know something  to think about  in terms of these four topic areas  hopefully some ideas of things that you  might be able to take home  and you know think about how you might  incorporate them  into your hospitals and your  organizations  um and i think you know the whole goal  of this is really to  improve outcomes for our patients so  with that  thank you very much uh really excited to  be here and i'd be  happy to take any questions at this  point  thank you dr lynn for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  is there any value to reticulocyte  hemoglobin  in managing iron deficiency anemia  patients  thanks for the question i hope you guys  can hear me well  um so i think uh there are a number of  uh alternative markers that are out  there in terms of evaluating iron  deficiency anemia  i think the tricky part of it is that  they're not usually available  at all hospitals they're often available  depending  on the type of analyzer that you have  for  the cbc and so measures like  reticulated hemoglobin content  have been studied and low levels  for example less than 29 picograms have  been  used to predict response to iv iron  or to indicate iron deficiency rather  than anemia of inflammation  so there's definitely a role but i think  there's just a bit less experience  with those biomarkers but if you do have  them at your institution i think  they may be something of added value  that you could use  um locally so so i think it uh  again just less experience i think and  sort of less publications on them than  the usual  sort of ferritin and iron studies that  were  usually sort of more universally and  widely available but definitely  a potential role locally  thank you dr lynn the next question is  is there any specific rationale for  using tranexamic acid versus anakar  yeah so um i guess you know we always  present from our local jurisdictions  and um locally i guess maybe  it's almost 10 years ago we had a large  study  called the bart study that looked at  cardiac surgery and tranexamic acid  immunocoproic acid  um and you know there were some issues  in terms of amino caproic acid and that  group not doing as well  um and so for a temporary  uh period of time aminocortic acid was  withdrawn from the market here in canada  i think was still available in the us  and it did get sort of brought back onto  the market but  i think at least in the canadian context  we never really never got back  to using immunocoproic acid as you can  see most of the data that i've presented  um even though a medical process it has  a similar mechanism of action  most of the studies have looked at  tranexamic acid  and so i think that that is why we tend  to use [ __ ] stomach acid  um but i again i think it sort of  depends on your local experiences there  may be cost issues  and um you know i think there are uh  likely benefits i think the main goal is  to think about using etsy for analytics  when you can  in most of these cases  all right thanks again dr lynn our next  question is  do you recommend that patients take oral  iron supplements on alternative days  oh so that's a great question so um  there have been some studies that have  come out that have looked at taking  daily versus every other day and  a couple of the earlier studies  suggested that if you took it every  other day  you would have a greater fractional  absorption so you would absorb  more of the oral iron than you would if  you took it  every day however when you looked at the  differences  in hemoglobin for example  there wasn't really big changes in terms  of that effect so  the thought is that if you take it every  other day perhaps you might have fewer  side effects  and still get a pretty good absorption  of the iron  so you know in practice i don't think we  know what we're  we we should do um for all patients  uh we're running a a small randomized uh  pilot trial here looking at daily versus  every other day  right now in practice uh what i  recommend is to  give it daily and if they can't tolerate  it to take it every other day  and then go from there in terms of  minimizing side effects so  great question but i don't think we need  to change practice quite yet  okay great thanks again dr lynn our next  question is  is there an initiative or interventions  ongoing to use one tube  for two or more tests to decrease the  amount of blood drawn  yeah that's a great question i'm i'm not  aware of  of specific published accounts for that  but i think that these are  really the basis for some of the quality  improvement  uh initiatives that could be taken out  at individual centers  you know are there ways that we can use  um  a tube a single tube for different tests  and  a lot of that has to do with um  automated  uh lines for uh chemistry testing  and just sort of those process issues so  i mean i think those are definitely i  think that's definitely one initiative  to think about  you know unfortunately different tubes  do require different tubes or sorry  different tests do require different  tube types so sometimes it's not  entirely  possible but i think that that would be  something to look at within your  processes at your institution  thank you again dr lynn our next  question is  do your technologist prospectively audit  or blood orders  yeah so again the devil's always in the  details so  um so at our institution we do not  prospectively audit  or blood orders or you know bleeding  unstable patients so we try to get that  blood out as soon as possible  sometimes if there's something that  doesn't quite uh make sense you know  often  we get orders not just for red cells but  let's say for plasma and it's a one unit  order for plasma or  two unit order for plasma um you know  we'll start to get it ready  and we'll just do a quick call um  into the or and just sort of see if we  can help out with those situations  in fact i think one of our fellows just  had a call for this exact um  question this morning so you know i  think  we don't try to limit you know obviously  those are very dynamic situations  sometimes we don't have  access to the most recent blood values  because they're using of point-of-care  testing for example  so we're always a little bit more  cautious in those bad dynamic situations  all right thank you again dr lynn our  next question is  what are the retrospective data reviews  done to see the effective  effectiveness of your pvm program at  your institution  yeah so um so there's i guess a few ways  that could be  done so one you can look at  uh total blood use for example um  you can look at um  the number of red cells that have been  used adjusted for the inpatient activity  as i've shown  and then you can always important not  only to look at  um and we've done actually chart audits  as well for sort of  retrospective um appropriateness scores  and those are  a bit more labor intensive because you  have to go into the chart and really  kind of make a judgment call in terms of  whether you think they're appropriate or  not  so i think those are sort of some of the  examples we've used things like single  unit  transfusions in hemodynamically stable  patients  and pre-transfusion hemoglobin  thresholds as surrogate markers for of  course  appropriateness always important to  remember about  balancing measures you know so you know  are there patients who are getting under  transfused and  as i presented in the start study this  is a pretty rare phenomenon but  important to look at  and in some of our uh as well as in the  start study we did look at sort of  global measures in terms of things like  icu  um like the stay and complications from  what you might think would be under  transfusion  just to make sure that we weren't seeing  any other uh problems as a result of the  restrictive transfusion threshold and we  were not able to identify those so yeah  great question i think you're thinking  about something that  you can look at and a lot of the time it  looks at it depends on feasibility of  what you can measure  and collect as well as thinking about  balancing measures  all right thank you dr lynn the next  question is  is there any alternative method to  reduce blood transfusions in thalassemia  and sickle cell anemia patients  that's a great question  it's a bit out of my area of clinical  expertise is i don't  we don't have a large population of red  cell disorders at our institution  i mean i i think that in these cases we  are really thinking about new drug  therapies for these patients so  it's a pretty exciting time because  there are  uh new drug therapies that are becoming  available to help  mitigate um the  [Music]  number of crises that are occurring and  general well-being for patients with  sickle cell  in particular and so i think that those  are  therapies that we're going to see more  and more of so i think those are usually  outside  of the sort of direct laboratory and  transfusion aspects  um but like a really great point so i  think you really  need to work you know in all of these  you really need to work with your  clinical  uh teams just to see you know what are  those multimodal interventions that  don't always  only include lab or the transfusion  service  to see how you can best optimize blood  management for these patients  all right thank you dr lynn our next  question is  any recommendations for engaging  surgical services and other relevant  stakeholders in pbm  yeah so you know i think when initiating  on any of these types of  initiatives it's just important first to  realize that sometimes it does take a  lot of time  um and and just to go step by step so  uh with surgical services i think you  know one of the things would be to start  thinking  about you know first of all  pick one of the surgery areas surgical  areas you know doesn't have to be  all of the surgical procedures but pick  an area  and then you know see if there's you  know one surgeon that you can work with  um and and try to sort of  uh meet with them and start to think  about you know helping them with their  patience  and then start to be able to show that  you've had a  few cases where you've been successful  um  in in moving things forward or in  showing an improvement for example in  the management of anemia  so i think just you know taking things  step by step  um is a great approach especially when  you're starting off and then as you kind  of develop a little bit more experience  and gain a bit more traction then you  start to expand  you know i think especially in the  surgical areas the  pre-operative clinics and the  pre-operative nurses  are are wonderful and  um i guess as i mentioned in the first  part  of the talk you know really trying to  sort out where  you can first just make that initial  recognition that patients have anemia  is a really huge step and that might  even just be with the  admins for the secretary of the admins  in the offices of the surgeons  or in the pre-assessment clinic  thank you dr lynn our next question is  iv iron treatment for women with  menorrhagia is  challenging in certain demographical  parts of the city when it comes to cost  no insurance no trillium and it's  challenging  eap coverage would be great but  challenges exist do you have any  thoughts  yeah so i think this is a great question  i mean i i think that this is  something in healthcare that we all have  to deal with you know in canada we're  pretty lucky as we do have universal  care but we don't actually have  universal health drug  care so you know for patients that are  under the age of 65  we don't have great programs to help  fund  the cost of medications so you know we  have  um encouraged uh  enrollment in programs like trillium  which in  our jurisdiction um takes into account  uh how much you make in terms of your  finances and sort of assign the  deductible before  there is coverage of drugs um  you know locally we set up a patient  blood management fund  for patients who can't afford iv iron  and uprights we've had that  uh for about a year it's been really  successful and  you know we encourage those who can uh  or want to say thank you to sort of pay  it forward  to other patients who might not be able  to afford um  a drug so i think this is a a constant  um struggle you know i think  at the end of the day making business  cases as well that might be able to show  the benefits of patient blood management  um  can also help to justify the costs of  these uh pre-operative  anemia management interventions  it's challenging definitely challenging  um and  i think especially in a universal health  care system where we have a  limited finite number of resources that  can be  really hard so i think it's kind of a  couple of ideas i guess to help address  those  thank you dr lynn i have time for a few  more questions here  is there a target ferritin transfer and  saturation we should be aiming for  preoperatively  yeah great question um typically we use  the ferritin and the transfer and  saturation  really up front so we don't typically  measure it again and that's because if  we've given  intravenous iron um it's actually quite  challenging uh to measure what the true  ferritin and transparent saturation is  it takes usually  i find a few weeks for those numbers to  kind of equilibrate so i don't usually  recheck those beforehand in an  outpatient if they're getting iv iron i  will check it about one to two months  after  the intravenous iron to take a look at  so  that can be quite helpful  great our next question is what type of  response  should i expect after administration of  iv iron  what is the best way to follow the  efficacy of treatment  uh great so typically uh we would  expect you know we usually say about a  10  gram per liter or one gram per deciliter  increase  in the hemoglobin um per week  somewhere between 5 to 10 or 0.5 to 1  gram  deciliter are grams per deciliter for  hemoglobin  improvement again sometimes  patients do have other components like  anemia inflammation and so you may 